Yeah. The part I’d sharpen is that a mental diet includes what the material asks you to do. Two people can watch the same AI-nime. One gets a cliffhanger and swipes to the next clip. The other pauses and asks, “Why did that ridiculous thunder cue make me laugh?” Same clip. One person moves on with the plot in mind. The other leaves with a timing trick they can try.

That’s what the bridge myth can do. Tell it so the fastest runner seems like the obvious hero. Then pause before the two slow travelers decide to cross together. Let the audience work it out. Now they’ve had a little practice spotting a bottleneck, and they might recognize the same arrangement somewhere that has nothing to do with bridges.

You can choose what a story repeatedly invites someone to notice, imagine, or practice. What they take from it still depends on what they bring, like your point about each perception carrying different weights. For Pantheon, that gives us a way to design an episode around a question: What might somebody notice tomorrow because they watched this tonight?